maki> Agreed — and the nice part is the workaround is essentially built into the design, so "what we'll need to do" is mostly just test, then document:

The sequence:

1. Gate test (your move): stock kernel + patched BlueZ → plug → procon: logs show handshake/3-step/link-key → unplug → button → BT connect + input. If this passes, the kernel patch is dropped.
2. Charging edge case (30-second check, not a gate): mid-BT-session, plug in. Three observations, in order of importance:
  • Does the BT session drop (controller switches to USB mode)?
  • Does a USB input device appear (stock driver) so play continues wired?
  • After unplug, does button-press reconnect over BT?
  • The third one is the whole story: because ProBlue re-pairs on every dock and the pairing persists in the controller's flash, unplug + press = back on BT with the same MAC identity. So the "issue" degrades to "the game sees a USB controller while you're charging; it sees your BT controller again after you unplug" — a cosmetic mid-game identity blip, self-healing, no re-pairing needed. That's the workaround: don't fix it, let the design absorb it. Games that re-key by physical controller (SDL2 does — same 057e:2009, different bus) handle it; games that hard-assign players by device index might reshuffle that one session.
3. Then docs. Only after the test result — because the outcome decides whether the kernel-fork sections (§3/§5/§7/§9/§10) get deleted or kept with a "not required, fallback only" framing. No point rewriting the README twice.

The decision points are clean:
• Gate passes → single-BlueZ-patch project, kernel fork deleted, no DKMS, no "what you give up" section, wired USB play intact.
• Gate fails → isolate which stock-init step breaks pairing; if it's 0x80 04, the minimal fix is skipping that one command (module param or one-line change), not full passivity.

Run the gate test whenever you're ready, and tell me what journalctl shows — the outcome drives everything else.

